---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../../data/site';
import { measurements, environment, decomposition, perfSchedAvailable, fine } from '../../data/measurements';
import AccuracyLog from '../../components/charts/AccuracyLog.astro';
import PairedRuns from '../../components/charts/PairedRuns.astro';
import CompareMatrix from '../../components/charts/CompareMatrix.astro';

const gated = measurements.filter((m) => !m.skipped);
const reported = measurements.filter((m) => m.skipped);
---

<Base
  title="Research — the method, the evidence, and where the claim stops"
  description="How PACE's attribution is measured, what it was checked against, and the parts of the claim that do not survive contact with the incumbents."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href={href()}>PACE</a> / research</div>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">Research · the other half of this site</div>
      <h1>The evidence, published next to the marketing rather than behind it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This is not a company describing a product it wants you to trust. It is one maintainer
        publishing a measurement and the checks on it, including the checks that are weaker than they
        look and the claims that were withdrawn. The marketing pages sell the thing; these pages are
        the reason to believe it, and they are the reason to disbelieve parts of it.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href={href('research/accuracy/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-ruler"></i>
          The accuracy method
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={href('research/prior-art/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-scales"></i>
          Prior art
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${site.repo}/blob/main/pace-sdk/results/accuracy.json`}>
          <i class="ph ph-file-code"></i>
          The raw artifact
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The method in one paragraph</div>
      <h2>Measure the same window two ways and compare</h2>
      <p>
        PACE's claim is that its split of a thread's wall time is real. The way to check that is not
        to reason about it: it is to run PACE and an independent source over the same thread and the
        same window, and compare the numbers. The verification harness does that with the kernel's own
        scheduling accounting, with <span class="mono">perf sched timehist</span>, and with an
        independently read <span class="mono">CLOCK_MONOTONIC</span>. Every figure on this site comes
        out of that harness and lands in one committed JSON file.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-arrows-left-right"></i> Paired runs</h3>
          <p>
            {gated.reduce((n, m) => n + m.n, 0)} comparisons across the gated checks, plus
            {' '}{decomposition.samples.toLocaleString('en-US')} recorded samples for the invariant.
            Both sources are read over the same window as PACE.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-database"></i> Two observed regimes</h3>
          <p>
            A <span class="mono">spin</span> window where the thread stays runnable, and a
            <span class="mono">sleep</span> window where it is off-CPU almost entirely. A measurement
            that only worked in one of them would not be a measurement.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-lock"></i> A gate per check</h3>
          <p>
            Each comparison has a threshold, the build fails when one is missed, and the thresholds
            and their basis are in the artifact rather than in prose.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <AccuracyLog />
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:-8px">
        <a class="btn" href={href('research/accuracy/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-list-magnifying-glass"></i>
          Every figure, and the method behind it
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The environment</div>
      <h2>One machine, stated so you can discount for it</h2>
      <p>
        Every number here comes from one laptop. That is a real limitation on how far the figures
        generalise, and the honest way to publish it is to publish the machine alongside the result.
      </p>
      <div class="readout">
        <div class="readout-head">
          <span>the measurement machine</span>
          <span class="mono">pace-sdk/results/accuracy.json · environment</span>
        </div>
        <div class="readout-body">
          <div class="status-list">
            <dt>cpu</dt><dd>{environment.cpu_model}</dd>
            <dt>threads</dt><dd>{environment.cpu_count}</dd>
            <dt>kernel</dt><dd class="mono">{environment.uname}</dd>
            <dt>governor</dt><dd class="mono">{environment.governor}</dd>
            <dt>perf_event_paranoid</dt><dd class="mono">{environment.kernel_perf_event_paranoid} — the strict default</dd>
            <dt>clk_tck</dt><dd class="mono">{environment.clk_tck}</dd>
            <dt>recorded</dt><dd class="mono">{environment.timestamp_utc}</dd>
            <dt>perf sched available</dt><dd>{perfSchedAvailable ? 'yes' : 'no'}</dd>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PairedRuns />
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">What a single-machine result does and does not support</div>
        <p>
          It supports: the split is measured rather than modelled, the arithmetic holds exactly, and
          the agreement with the kernel's own accounting is not an artefact of agreement between two
          of PACE's own numbers. It does not support: the same percentages on a different kernel, a
          different scheduler or a different architecture. Re-running the harness is one command, and
          the harness is in the repository precisely so that the second machine is somebody else's
          problem to solve.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The claim, and its limits</div>
      <h2>What was withdrawn, and what replaced it</h2>
      <p>
        The premise this project started from was that no existing tool separates time-on-CPU from
        time-lost-to-preemption. <strong>That premise was false.</strong> Intel VTune's threading
        analysis already does it on Linux. The claim was rewritten to something narrower, and the
        rewrites are recorded rather than quietly edited:
      </p>
      <CompareMatrix />
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-x-circle"></i> Withdrawn</h3>
          <p>
            "No existing tool separates work from pause." Withdrawn, because VTune does.
          </p>
          <p>
            "PACE ships for Linux, Windows and macOS with parity." Withdrawn: it ships for Linux
            today, and the other two platforms exist behind the same API with the split marked
            unavailable, which is not parity.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-check-circle"></i> Replaced with</h3>
          <p>
            Portable, embeddable, privilege-free and in-process. VTune cannot be linked into a
            third-party game engine, and Perfetto is a trace rather than a counter — no buffer, no
            IPC, no background writer.
          </p>
          <p>
            The verified core is the <em>time</em> attribution. The cycle figures are a labelled
            convenience, computed from a measured frequency, and the API marks them as an estimate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The full competitive and prior-art analysis, including the patent posture and the searches
        that were and were not run, is on
        {' '}<a href={href('research/prior-art/')}>the prior-art page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Reproduce it</div>
      <h2>The harness is in the repository, and it is the point</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-play"></i> Run the accuracy harness</h3>
          <pre><code>{`cd PACE/pace-sdk
cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja
cmake --build build -j 8
python3 tests/verify/verify_accuracy.py \\
  --out results/accuracy.json`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It needs the kernel's scheduler statistics switched on to check the run-queue delay, and
            it records the environment it ran in next to the numbers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-eye"></i> What to look for when you do</h3>
          <p>
            The decomposition should be exact and not approximate: a violation count of zero, and not
            a small percentage. If you see a nonzero violation count, that is a bug worth reporting
            and it is the one failure mode this project treats as a defect rather than as noise.
          </p>
          <p>
            The {reported.length} reported-not-gated checks are labelled as such in the artifact with
            a <span class="mono">skip_reason</span>. Not every comparison is a gate, and pretending
            otherwise would be the easiest lie on this site.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The tightest gated figure is {fine(Math.min(...gated.map((m) => m.median)))} and the loosest
        gate is set at {fine(Math.max(...gated.map((m) => m.threshold)))}. Both come from the
        artifact, as does every other number on this page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
